From d7702397cf2093e6e48c5a858ebdc082477e44c6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 13:12:24 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08095/11362] [outer-loop] Inert-rejection A/B harness +
 pre-registration, and the adversarial pass over it

The harness for measuring what CARNOT_ARC_INDUCE_REJECT_INERT costs. Committed
BEFORE the numbers exist, so the pre-registration is a contract rather than a
description: 20 games (the object-perception roster, reused unchanged so it
cannot have been picked to favour the treatment), 4 replicates, one seeded
llama-server for both arms, sign test clustered at the GAME.

POWER IS STATED UP FRONT AND IT IS NOT GOOD. The treatment can only fire where
an attempt-0 engine is clean AND inert (base rate 12-15%), and on the primary a
fired cell only creates a discordance if the re-ask returns junk. Expected
primary-discordant games: 2-3. Six are needed for p<0.05. The pre-registration
says in as many words that the primary probably cannot reach 0.05, and that a
null there means "no LARGE cost detected", not "no cost".

THE ADVERSARIAL PASS FOUND FOUR REAL THINGS, all fixed here:

  1. score_worker.py's docstring claimed the state-graph probe was "COPIED
     VERBATIM" from the metric-validity run. It was not: one output field was
     renamed, `engine_changes_anything_at_root` (itself a reported predictor at
     AUC 0.694) was dropped, and the error field was reshaped. Being the same
     code is the entire value of quoting that measure, so the code is restored
     and `test_probe_is_the_measure_that_was_validated` now compares the two
     functions' executable ASTs and fails the build on divergence.
  2. The mechanistic witness defined a treatment TRIGGER as `inert`, but the
     treatment's own condition is `clean AND inert` -- so an engine that raises
     on everything would have counted as a trigger, and the correct absence of
     a re-ask would have read as a treatment failure.
  3. The pre-registration calls a truncated completion MISSING; the collection
     code scores it as the defect `validate_engine_code` reports. Both are now
     computed and reported side by side, because picking one after seeing the
     numbers is the offence.
  4. "Ships inert" was asserted on the RETURN VALUE only. That would still hold
     if the probe ran on every induction and its finding were discarded -- a
     subprocess spawn per induction on the scored path, wearing an identical
     result. Now asserted as behaviour: the probe must not be CALLED.

Also pinned: the outcome definition is not the treatment (an inert engine must
stay CLEAN under `validate_engine_code`, or arm B wins by construction), the
analysis collapses replicates to per-game means before pairing, and a missing
observation is dropped rather than scored zero.

The A/A floor's reference point (1 of 4 byte-identical on the sibling run) was
verified against that run's rows.json rather than quoted from memory.

No default flipped. No scored or online ARC game played.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JntPrUGLNesPcJH1pyfFYw

ADDENDUM, found while collection was already running and recorded rather than
quietly fixed:

  * `ruff format <directory>` reformatted the five COLLECTED ENGINE FILES in
    out/engines/, so every recorded engine_sha256 stopped matching its file. The
    files were recoverable because e3_store/ is gitignored and ruff honours
    .gitignore; all five were restored from there and re-verified by hash. A
    local ruff.toml now excludes out/, and says in the config that the real rule
    is "never point a formatter at a directory holding collected artifacts" --
    a config can only defend the paths someone thought to list.
  * The seeded sampler DOES NOT REPRODUCE on real induce prompts. The first
    three (game, replicate) pairs all drew different engines across arms at
    byte-identical prompt hashes, identical seeds, and one completion call each.
    A direct probe of the same server (two identical short requests, fixed seed)
    came back byte-identical, and the server runs a single slot with no
    --parallel, so the seed is honoured and this is not batch nondeterminism;
    `cache_prompt: true` prefix reuse is the leading hypothesis, untested. This
    contradicts `sampling_seed`'s docstring ("an A/A arm should come back
    byte-identical"). The consequence is stated in analyse.py: the pairing is a
    BLOCKING factor, not a matched sample, so this is a valid randomized A/B
    that is noisier and less powerful than the design assumed.
